{
  "id": "Compound::DB06237 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Avanafil, a phosphodiesterase inhibitor, is linked to Cyp3a4 through a shared mechanism involving Tadalafil. Both Avanafil and Tadalafil, as members of the Cgmp phosphodiesterase inhibitor class, bind to Cyp3a4, suggesting a common target for these compounds. The evidence indicates that Avanafil causes Tadalafil, which in turn binds to Cyp3a4, establishing a causal pathway from Avanafil to Cyp3a4. This relationship is supported by the shared lowest common ancestor (LCA) of Avanafil and Tadalafil, both belonging to the Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound class. The integration of these paths highlights a potential mechanism where Avanafil's effects are mediated through Tadalafil, which interacts with Cyp3a4, underscoring a functional link between the drug and the enzyme. The evidence suggests that Avanafil may influence Cyp3a4 through Tadalafil, potentially affecting drug metabolism or other physiological processes.",
